{
  "result": "I think a lot about the Museum of Lost Things. Not a real building, of course, but the imaginary archive we all carry. It's where the other sock goes, where single earrings and childhood drawings and the exact taste of your grandmother's soup end up. It's meticulously cataloged, even if we can't find the door.\n\nWe lose about two thousand small things in a lifetime, if you count keys and pens and umbrellas. But the heavier inventory is invisible: the names of kids we went to elementary school with, the lyrics to songs we swore we'd never forget, the feeling of being seven and believing summer would never end. They sit on dusty shelves in that museum.\n\nI like to imagine wandering its halls at night. In one wing, there's every letter I meant to send but never did. In another, all the alternate versions of myself \u2014 the one who learned the guitar, the one who said yes, the one who stayed. They stand there, not judging, just existing.\n\nThe strange comfort of this museum is that nothing is truly gone. It has simply been moved to a different kind of storage. And sometimes, when the light hits a kitchen table just right or a familiar smell drifts in from a passing car, a curator pulls an item from the shelf and returns it to you, briefly, perfectly intact. For a second, you remember.\n\nAnd then, it is filed away again. That is enough to keep wandering, and to keep losing.",
